{"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"Both items resolved. Here's where things stand.\n\n## The GitGuardian alert — root cause found, not a regression\n\nYour redaction system and fixtures are intact — the corpus file that triggered the alert is **byte-identical to what's been on cledger's public `main` all along**. The actual flaw was in the original splitting scheme, and publishing annals as a *new* repo is what got it a fresh scan: two Slack-format positives in `secret-corpus.json` stored the token as prefix+body, so `secret_parts[0]` — 20 characters starting with `xox…-` — was a complete match for public scanners *on its own*. The split convention's whole premise is that fragments are inert; for that token family, that split wasn't.\n\nWhat I did (without ever printing a value into this conversation): re-split both entries mid-prefix with the reassembled values verified unchanged by sha256, added a corpus test asserting **no individual `secret_parts` fragment matches any common public detector shape** (so this can't regress quietly), ran the suite green (151), and pushed the fix to annals.\n\nWhat's left for you: the GitGuardian incident itself — these are synthetic fixture tokens, nothing real leaked, so close it as a test credential. Two honest caveats: the old fragments remain in annals' and cledger's git *history* (a history purge would be performative unless you rewrite both repos, which synthetic tokens don't warrant), and cledger `main`'s tip still carries the old corpus until PR #11 merges — so GitGuardian could conceivably ping once more on cledger activity before the merge.\n\n## Turnbridge — yes, hand it to that agent\n\nAgreed. The remaining items are turnbridge-internal design work (its `TODO_HANDOFF.md` section B: reading `producer.model`/`provider` instead of `actor.id`, marker reduction, disclosure placement, and the big one — per-turn \"copy from the original, never from the copy\" multi-hop rendering). Those need turnbridge's tier-policy docs in conte