{"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"Sanity checks done along the way — all three suites ran green after their final changes (annals 150, cledger 90, turnbridge 91), and each repo's work is committed on its own branch. Here's the full picture.\n\n## Your question: fold turnbridge into cledger once annals exists?\n\n**Keep them separate — your own reasoning cuts the other way once the store is out.** The rule you stated (\"effectively separate systems in code → separately installable\") is exactly why cledger/annals split, and it's why cledger/turnbridge shouldn't merge: post-split, cledger is *vocabulary + capture + views* (~5,500 lines: adapters for five CLIs, the kind definitions, log/show/export, renormalize) and turnbridge is *fabrication + lineage* (~4 target renderers, the tier policy, `continuation`). They share almost no code — turnbridge consumes cledger's reader the way context-graph will. The dependency chain is cleanly one-way (`turnbridge → cledger → annals`), and merging would force every future context-graph/intent-recall install to drag in four CLI fabrication adapters they'll never call. The two do share *session-format knowledge* (cledger parses the formats turnbridge fabricates), which is a real coupling — but it's spec-level, not code-level, and turnbridge's `docs/specs/` already serves both as the shared authority.\n\n## What I built (all local branches, nothing pushed)\n\n**`~/repos/evintunador/annals`** — new repo, initial commit on `main`. The extracted store: append/read, identity+dedup, commit anchoring, re-anchoring, redaction-on-write, secret scan, review TUI, transport (hook + refspec), forge suggestions. Generalized: schema `annals/v1`, ids `ev2-`, `conversation` → **`stream`**, and **namespaces** — every operation takes a `Ledger` from `openLedger(repo, nsConfig)`, where the namespace picks the notes ref, state dir, config file, and hook CLI. That's the mechanism intent-recall's \"own append-only log on a separate ref\" needs. `readEvents` gained the `tool` / `kind[]` / `linkRel` filters from the old handoff doc. Minimal `annals` CLI (`sync`, `transport-push`) so a bare install's hook works. 150 tests.\n\n**`conversation-ledger`** branch `annals-extraction` (0.26.0) — cledger sits on `file:../annals`. Its namespace keeps every on-disk name you have today (`refs/notes/conversation-ledger`, `.cledger.json`, `~/.config/cledger`, the installed hooks upgrade themselves in place). Old module paths remain as thin wrappers, and `ConversationRef` survives as an alias, so consumers keep compiling. 90 tests.\n\n**`turnbridge`** branch `annals-cledger` — mechanical `conversation` → `stream` follow-up. 91 tests.\n\n## The one thing left for you\n\nEvent ids changed (ev2) and old ev1 events don't match the new envelope, so existing ledger history should be wiped as you preferred. I wrote `scripts/wipe-ev1-history.sh` (on the cledger branch) covering all nine repos, **dry-run by default** — I have not run it. When you're ready, in a plain terminal:\n\n```\nsh scripts/wipe-ev1-history.sh            # preview\nsh scripts/wipe-ev1-history.sh --execute  # deletes local + origin notes refs\n```\n\nIt keeps capture cursors (so old transcripts don't re-backfill), allowlists, and known-secrets. Also: the annals repo has no GitHub remote yet, and I left all pushing to you since the pre-push scan is a human checkpoint by your own project rules. The retired `design/extension-governance` branch can be deleted whenever — its live ideas (pair-key identification, the three filters, transport disclosure) all landed in annals' README and code.\n\nresult: Built the split — new `annals` package (git-notes record store: ev2/stream envelope, per-producer namespaces, tool/kind[]/linkRel filters; 150 tests), cledger 0.26.0 rewired onto it (90 tests) and turnbridge fixed up (91 tests), all on local unpushed branches, with a dry-run wipe script for the old ev1 history left for the human to execute; recommended cledger and turnbridge stay separate packages.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"cleanup/annals-extraction-debt","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","head":"6db8b27b11f6bb207423cc2df7e9f08e7dfe6cdb","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"id":"ev2-859564f5f2ca91550519d8eb0bc95ac6378dc9102c3b8ad2bc8d3af2b9de2964","kind":"conversation_turn","meta":{"actor":{"type":"agent"},"model":"gpt-5.6-sol","provider":"openai","session_id":"331d6dd2-a7e9-4695-8b0e-9b329a3e5e00","source":"codex","source_version":"0.156.1"},"occurred_at":"2026-09-01T22:57:31.500Z","producer":{"tool":"cledger","version":"0.26.0"},"raw":{"data":{"payload":{"content":[{"text":"Sanity checks done along the way — all three suites ran green after their final changes (annals 150, cledger 90, turnbridge 91), and each repo's work is committed on its own branch. Here's the full picture.\n\n## Your question: fold turnbridge into cledger once annals exists?\n\n**Keep them separate — your own reasoning cuts the other way once the store is out.** The rule you stated (\"effectively separate systems in code → separately installable\") is exactly why cledger/annals split, and it's why cledger/turnbridge shouldn't merge: post-split, cledger is *vocabulary + capture + views* (~5,500 lines: adapters for five CLIs, the kind definitions, log/show/export, renormalize) and turnbridge is *fabrication + lineage* (~4 target renderers, the tier policy, `continuation`). They share almost no code — turnbridge consumes cledger's reader the way context-graph will. The dependency chain is cleanly one-way (`turnbridge → cledger → annals`), and merging would force every future context-graph/intent-recall install to drag in four CLI fabrication adapters they'll never call. The two do share *session-format knowledge* (cledger parses the formats turnbridge fabricates), which is a real coupling — but it's spec-level, not code-level, and turnbridge's `docs/specs/` already serves both as the shar